#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204678} :activity/id #uuid "112e7843-d752-5247-952e-8d7e1f2115f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050670 :topic/id #uuid "5da1bf0d-a741-59f5-981a-d9099d93b347" :topic/math-academy-id 4818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205241 :tutorial/content "The multiplicative inverse of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the unique number $x$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nThe word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x < n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nis the multiplicative inverse of\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{6}}\n$$\n\nmodulo $23$ since\n$6 \\cdot 4\\begin{vmatrix}≡24 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡23 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡0 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡1 & (mod23)\\end{vmatrix}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes denote the multiplicative inverse of $a$ as $a^{-1}$. So, ${\\color{blue}{6}}^{-1} = {\\color{red}{4}}$ in this example.\n- Be careful not confuse the notation $a^{-1}$ with the reciprocal of $a$. In this context, both $a$ and $a^{-1}$ are *integers.*\n- The uniqueness of $a^{-1}$ is up to congruence modulo $n$. For example, since ${\\color{red}{27}}\\equiv {\\color{red}{4}}\\:(\\textrm{mod}23)$, we have\n$27 \\cdot 6\\begin{vmatrix}≡162 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡7 \\cdot 23 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡7 \\cdot 0 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡1 & (mod23)\\end{vmatrix}$.\n\nThere are circumstances when a multiplicative inverse does not exist. To this end, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1$.*\n\nFor example:\n\n- We saw earlier that $6^{-1} = 4$ modulo $23$. Indeed, $\\textrm{gcd}(6,23) = 1$. Moreover, since $n=23$ is prime, *every* integer $1 \\leq x \\leq 22$ modulo $23$ has a multiplicative inverse. More on that in future lessons.\n- In contrast, the integer $a=2$ has no multiplicative inverse modulo $14$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(2,14) = 2\\neq 1$. Intuitively, multiplying $a = 2$ by any integer $x$ modulo $14$ gives an even number, and taking the result modulo $14$ (also even) will never give us $1$ (an odd integer)." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f17a19d-6a8b-5bc3-8791-dea4766e234f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9080 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a5b99501-7340-5057-8ee9-0baaede0bec8" :step/math-academy-id 154718 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203702 :question/id #uuid "b0bec0e5-41ab-591f-ab80-5e43602ff808" :question/math-academy-id "e-14656"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048141}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204252}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"} :step/id #uuid "c2284103-2148-5b46-8a53-3a722079cfc6" :step/math-academy-id 154719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205255 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$. In other words, we want to find a number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\nFirstly, we note that since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(3,14) = 1\n$$\n\na unique multiplicative inverse exists.\n\nSince the modulus $n=14$ is fairly small, one way to efficiently compute the inverse is to use trial and error. To do this, we compute powers of $3$ and take the residue modulo $14$ until we get a result of $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3^{2} & ≡3 \\cdot 3≡9 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{3} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{2}≡3 \\cdot 9≡27≡1 \\cdot 14 + 13≡13 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{4} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{3}≡3 \\cdot 13≡39≡2 \\cdot 14 + 11≡11 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{5} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{4}≡3 \\cdot 11≡33≡2 \\cdot 14 + 5≡5 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{6} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{5}≡3 \\cdot 5≡15≡1 \\cdot 14 + 1≡1 & (mod14)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAccording to the last line, we have\n$3 \\cdot {\\color{magenta}{5}} \\equiv 1\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)$.\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$ is ${\\color{magenta}{5}}$, and we can write\n\n$$\n3^{-1} = {\\color{magenta}{5}}\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could get the same result by substituting\n\n$$\nx=1,2,\\ldots, 13\n$$\n\ninto\n$3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)$\nuntil we get the correct congruence relation. However, the exponentiation method described above typically allows us to find the solution with fewer trials since the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (if exists) must appear among the powers of $a$ modulo $n$." :tutorial/id #uuid "13c4f6aa-637a-5936-8a92-312c2b9e9a0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9128 :tutorial/title "Finding a Multiplicative Inverse Using Trial and Error"} :step/id #uuid "ed3a5a48-8516-5eeb-930d-4d076964c2c8" :step/math-academy-id 154722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203703 :question/id #uuid "060283e9-0fbd-523b-bc30-37936fabacf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14657"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d467de67-1ec6-57ab-98e7-8f7e9797d54d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050678}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204261}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using Exponentiation"} :step/id #uuid "9cb9d1be-4b8e-5874-a893-169b5428b666" :step/math-academy-id 154723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205249 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we want to compute the multiplicative inverse of $23$ modulo $79$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1\n$$\n\nthe inverse exists. However, the modulus $n=79$ is quite big, so finding the inverse by trial and error is inefficient. So, what do we do?\n\nTo answer this, recall that Bézout's identity states that there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = \\textrm{gcd}(23,79)\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = 1\n$$\n\nWe can find the integers $u$ and $v$ using the extended Euclidian algorithm.\n\nApplying the Euclidian algorithm (we'll skip over the details for now), we find that\n\n$$\nu=-24, v = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 = 1\n$$\n\nTo find the multiplicative inverse of $23$, the key is to write this result modulo $79$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 0 & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (-24) & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (0 - 24) & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (79 - 24) & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot 55 & ≡1 & (mod79)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $23 \\cdot 55 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:79)$. Therefore, $55$ is the multiplicative inverse of $23$ (modulo $79$).\n\nFinally, we can express this result using the residue function as follows:\n\n$$\n23^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 79 = 55\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "447cbeda-3f33-51d7-89d9-c3319b9a824a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9110 :tutorial/title "Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"} :step/id #uuid "626236a3-449d-507a-ad80-1e62de4bb150" :step/math-academy-id 154726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203914 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203704 :question/id #uuid "15204e14-a2a1-5591-8448-1864066a29b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14658"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc98cf4e-55f1-5fbd-a779-fb400adf1b44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049094}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204275}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Inverses Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm"} :step/id #uuid "530b8975-4ba9-555d-90ab-d04c615953e6" :step/math-academy-id 154727}] :activity/title "Multiplicative Inverses Modulo N" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204110}]}
